      "id": 1,
      "text": "Pawnee City was incorporated in 1858 and named after the Pawnee Native Americans.",
      "sourceSentenceIds": [
        4
      ],
      "sourceLabels": [
        "Wikipedia"
      ],
      "source": "https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Pawnee_City%2C_Nebraska",
      "category": "etymology",
      "quality": "good"
    },
    {
      "id": 2,
      "text": "By the 1880s, Pawnee City had become a railroad town at the junction of two railroad lines.",
      "sourceSentenceIds": [
        5
      ],
      "sourceLabels": [
        "Wikipedia"
      ],
      "source": "https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Pawnee_City%2C_Nebraska",
      "category": "history",
      "quality": "good"
    },
    {
      "id": 3,
      "text": "A devastating fire on August 9, 1881 destroyed two‑thirds of downtown, after which the town rebuilt with fireproof construction.",
      "sourceSentenceIds": [
        19,
        20
      ],
      "sourceLabels": [
        "Wikipedia"
      ],
      "source": "https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Pawnee_City%2C_Nebraska",
      "category": "history",
      "quality": "good"
    },
    {
      "id": 4,
      "text": "The town’s historic downtown is known for its quirky museums and Indigenous heritage, drawing history buffs and car enthusiasts alike.",
      "sourceSentenceIds": [
        10,
        12
      ],
      "sourceLabels": [
        "Web source 2"
      ],
      "source": "https://www.islands.com/1985605/nebraska-pawnee-city-town-historic-downtown-museums-native-roots/",
      "category": "culture",
      "quality": "good"
    },
    {
      "id": 5,
      "text": "Periodic reenactments celebrate Pawnee City’s rich history, while the local library offers a self‑guided itinerary for visitors.",
